== Vendor-match noise (pre-existing issue, not fixed this run) ==
src/normalize.py's match_vendors() does substring matching on lowercased
title+body against the FULL vendor list, including short tokens like "SEC"
and "SSE". This produces false-positive vendor hits on nearly every item
(e.g. "SEC" matches inside "security", "second", "sector"; the NVD RustFS
CVE description matched vendor "Egress" purely because its body contains the
word "regression"). Treated "SEC" and "SSE" as noise tokens throughout
clustering/scoring judgment below rather than trusting them literally. Did
not touch config/ or src/ per scope discipline — flagging for the user to
consider either dropping SEC/SSE as vendor tokens or requiring word-boundary
+ minimum-length matching in match_vendors().

== Clustering ==
Applied the four rules (CVE / canonical URL / fuzzy title >=85 within 48h /
shared vendor token within 24h) programmatically first, then corrected by
hand for two known failure modes of the literal ruleset:
  1. Over-merge: rule 4 (shared vendor + 24h window) glued together unrelated
     Microsoft stories (Windows 11 EOL, UEFI shim Secure Boot bypass, an
     Age of Empires II patch, and the Patch Tuesday roundup) into one cluster
     purely because they all mention "Microsoft" same-day. Split back into
     4 distinct stories — these are different incidents, not one.
     Same issue merged the Shark vacuum flaw + Splunk/Zoom patch story +
     Schneier's "Protecting Privacy in an AI Era" post via spurious
     AWS/Splunk substring hits — split apart.
     Also merged an AI-agent prompt-injection article with two unrelated NVD
     CVEs (WWBN AVideo, Wekan) via spurious "GitHub"/"Socket" substring hits
     in the CVE body text — split apart.
  2. Under-merge: the two Zoom account-takeover writeups (BleepingComputer,
     TheHackerNews) describe the same CVE-2026-53412 but their titles didn't
     clear the 85 fuzzy-match threshold, so the literal rule left them as
     separate clusters. Merged editorially — confirmed same CVE, same CVSS
     9.8, same affected products in both bodies. SecurityWeek's "Splunk, Zoom
     Patch Critical Vulnerabilities" bundles the same Zoom CVE alongside
     Splunk's own separate critical patches; kept all three as one pick since
     the source itself treats them as one patch-day story.

